      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "[376.8s]\n",
            "Hello there! Great to meet you. As we begin your journey into data structures, let's talk about one of my favorite tools: the **Directed Acyclic Graph**, or **DAG** for short.\n",
            "\n",
            "Don't let the jargon scare you. At its heart, a DAG is just a network where two very specific rules apply:\n",
            "\n",
            "1.  **It has direction:** The arrows point in only one way (from $A$ to $B$, not back). This means if you start at node $X$, you can always move forward, but you never have to move backward.\n",
            "2.  **No loops (The \"Acyclic\" part):** Because of that forward-only rule, you can never get stuck in a circle and go around forever. You will eventually reach the end of your path.\n",
            "\n",
            "### A Simple Concrete Example: Course Prerequisites\n",
            "\n",
            "Imagine you are planning your schedule for an entire semester at university. You need to know which classes you *must* take before you can take others.\n",
            "\n",
            "Let's say we map these requirements as a graph where each **node** is a class and every **arrow** means \"this course must be completed before that one.\"\n",
            "\n",
            "Here is a possible sequence:\n",
            "*   **Math 101** points to **Data Structures**.\n",
            "*   **Math 101** also points to **Calculus II**.\n",
            "*   **Data Structures** points to **Artificial Intelligence (AI)**.\n",
            "*   **Calculus II** points to **Advanced Algorithms**.\n",
            "\n",
            "If you draw this out, you might see arrows flowing like this:\n",
            "> Math 101 $\\rightarrow$ Data Structures $\\rightarrow$ AI  \n",
            ">               $\\searrow$        (gap) $\\nwarrow$  \n",
            ">             Calculus II $\\rightarrow$ Advanced Algorithms\n",
            "\n",
            "This entire structure is a **DAG**.\n",
            "\n",
            "*   It has **directed** edges because you can only move from an easier class to a harder one.\n",
            "*   It is **acyclic** (no cycles) because you could never go back and take Math 101 *after* AI. You must always follow the arrow forward until you reach courses with no outgoing arrows (like \"AI\" or \"Advanced Algorithms\" in this simplified view).\n",
            "\n",
            "### Why does this matter to you?\n",
            "\n",
            "As a CS student, understanding DAGs is crucial because almost everything in programming relies on these structures:\n",
            "*   **Build Orders:** When building software, some modules depend on others. A DAG ensures your build script knows exactly what to compile first so nothing breaks.\n",
            "*   **Task Scheduling:** Think of a workflow where one task triggers the next. If you have a loop (a cycle), the process hangs; with a DAG, it finishes cleanly.\n",
            "*   **Topological Sorts:** We use DAGs to create a strict ordering list (like a class schedule or a compilation order).\n",
            "\n",
            "Think of it simply as a map of dependencies where time only moves forward. If there's no loop, you can always figure out the correct order to do everything.\n",
            "\n",
            "Does that image make sense? Let me know if you'd like to try mapping out a specific scenario with us!\n"
          ]
        }
      ]
