      "source": [
        "### Questions\n",
        "Compare the two answers. Explain the role of: **role · context · constraints · output format · specificity**.\n",
        "\n",
        "**✍️ Answer:**\n",
        "\n",
        "Prompt A gives a long, structured, technical answer with the five requested sections (definition, example, adjacency matrix, application, Python code). Prompt B gives a shorter, simpler explanation built around one concrete example (e.g. course prerequisites or task scheduling) and no matrix or code.\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Role** (\"You are a university professor\") sets the persona: tone, level of expertise and teaching style.\n",
        "- **Context** (the audience — \"a first-year computer science student\") sets the level of detail and vocabulary. Same role, different audience → different answer.\n",
        "- **Constraints** (\"must contain 1–5\", \"simple language\", \"one concrete example\") limit what the model includes and excludes.\n",
        "- **Output format** (the numbered list of sections) shapes the structure of the answer, making it predictable and easy to check.\n",
        "- **Specificity**: the more precise the request, the less the model has to guess. Prompt A is very specific, so its output is complete and checkable. A vague prompt gives a generic answer whose content and length vary from run to run."
      ]
    },
