      },
      "source": [
        "sizes = downloaded_models().set_index(\"name\")[\"size_GB\"].to_dict()\n",
        "t = table.set_index(\"Model\")\n",
        "fast = t[\"Response time (s)\"].idxmin(); slow = t[\"Response time (s)\"].idxmax()\n",
        "t[\"total\"] = t[\"Code correctness /5\"] + t[\"Requirements met /5\"]\n",
        "best = t[\"total\"].idxmax()\n",
        "big = max((MODEL, MODEL2), key=lambda m: sizes.get(m, sizes.get(m + \":latest\", 0)))\n",
        "same = t[\"total\"].nunique() == 1\n",
        "\n",
        "ex7_answer = f\"\"\"\n",
        "- **Faster:** `{fast}` ({t.loc[fast, 'Response time (s)']} s vs {t.loc[slow, 'Response time (s)']} s for `{slow}`). Response time depends on the model size, the number of tokens generated ({t.loc[fast, 'Tokens']} vs {t.loc[slow, 'Tokens']}) and on whether the model was already loaded in memory.\n",
        "- **Better code:** {\"both models scored the same\" if same else f\"`{best}`\"} — correctness {t.loc[MODEL, 'Code correctness /5']}/5 vs {t.loc[MODEL2, 'Code correctness /5']}/5, requirements met {t.loc[MODEL, 'Requirements met /5']}/5 vs {t.loc[MODEL2, 'Requirements met /5']}/5 (`{MODEL}` vs `{MODEL2}`).\n",
        "- **More memory:** `{big}`, which has the larger weight file ({sizes.get(big, sizes.get(big + ':latest', float('nan')))} GB on disk). A loaded model uses roughly its file size in RAM/VRAM plus the context (KV cache); the exact numbers are measured in Part 16.\n",
        "- **For programming assistance** I would choose `{best}`: it produced the most correct and complete code{\"\" if best == fast else \", even though it is not the fastest\"}. Correct code matters more than a few seconds of latency for a coding assistant.\n",
        "- **Is the largest model necessarily the best? No.** A bigger model has more knowledge and usually reasons better, but it needs more memory, loads more slowly and generates fewer tokens per second. A smaller model that is better trained or tuned for code can match or beat it on a given task. The choice should be based on measurements on the target machine and task, not only on parameter count.\n",
        "\"\"\"\n",
