      "source": [
        "### Questions\n",
        "- Difference between an LLM and a multimodal model?\n",
        "- What additional information does the vision model receive?\n",
        "- Which applications could use local vision AI?\n",
        "\n",
        "**✍️ Answer:**\n",
        "\n",
        "- A (text) **LLM** takes only text tokens as input and produces text. A **multimodal model** also accepts other modalities, here images. It adds a **vision encoder** (a Vision Transformer, like CLIP/SigLIP) and a **projection layer** that turns the image into vectors in the LLM's embedding space (\"visual tokens\"). The language model then reads the image tokens together with the text prompt and generates the answer.\n",
        "- In addition to the prompt, the vision model receives **the image itself**: its pixels, sent as a base64-encoded string in the `images` field of the request. Inside the model these become hundreds of visual tokens describing objects, colours, positions and any visible text, which is what lets it describe the scene. Like text, the image never leaves the computer.\n",
        "- **Applications:** document analysis and OCR of invoices, forms or exam papers; industrial quality inspection on a production line; education (explaining diagrams, correcting handwritten exercises); medical-image pre-screening where data must stay inside the hospital; analysing surveillance or drone images; accessibility (describing images for visually impaired users); sorting personal photo libraries. All of these benefit from local inference: **private data stays on site, no cloud cost, and it works offline**."
      ]
    },
