      "source": [
        "### Discussion — the model-size trade-off\n",
        "```\n",
        "Model size ──► Quality\n",
        "           ──► Memory\n",
        "           ──► Latency\n",
        "           ──► Hardware requirements\n",
        "```\n",
        "Local model selection should be based on **actual measurements on the target machine**, not just public benchmark rankings.\n",
        "\n",
        "**✍️ Discussion** (generated from your measured numbers):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "id": "c094",
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/",
          "height": 215
        },
        "id": "c094",
        "outputId": "c55388fd-ed99-4017-8e76-5c5bfb67404d"
      },
      "source": [
        "b = bench_df.set_index(\"Model\")\n",
        "fastest = b[\"Tokens/s\"].idxmax(); slowest = b[\"Tokens/s\"].idxmin()\n",
        "lighter = b[\"Loaded size (GB)\"].idxmin(); heavier = b[\"Loaded size (GB)\"].idxmax()\n",
        "best_q = b[\"Quality /5\"].idxmax()\n",
        "on_gpu = (b[\"VRAM (GB)\"].fillna(0) > 0).any()\n",
        "\n",
        "def fmt(m, col, unit=\"\"):\n",
        "    return f\"{b.loc[m, col]:.2f}{unit}\"\n",
        "\n",
        "discussion = f\"\"\"\n",
        "On this machine (`{platform.system()}`, {round(__import__('psutil').virtual_memory().total / 1e9)} GB RAM, {\"GPU used\" if on_gpu else \"CPU only\"}):\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Speed:** `{fastest}` generates {fmt(fastest, 'Tokens/s')} tokens/s vs {fmt(slowest, 'Tokens/s')} for `{slowest}`. Response time: {fmt(MODEL, 'Response time (s)', ' s')} (`{MODEL}`) vs {fmt(MODEL2, 'Response time (s)', ' s')} (`{MODEL2}`), including a cold load of {fmt(MODEL, 'Load time (s)', ' s')} and {fmt(MODEL2, 'Load time (s)', ' s')}.\n",
        "- **Memory:** `{heavier}` occupies {fmt(heavier, 'Loaded size (GB)', ' GB')} once loaded vs {fmt(lighter, 'Loaded size (GB)', ' GB')} for `{lighter}` (disk: {fmt(heavier, 'Disk size (GB)', ' GB')} vs {fmt(lighter, 'Disk size (GB)', ' GB')}). The loaded size is larger than the file because of the context (KV cache) and runtime buffers.\n",
        "- **Quality:** best automatic score for `{best_q}` ({b.loc[best_q, 'Quality /5']}/5 on the prime-number task of Exercise 7).\n",
        "\n",
        "**Trade-off.** More parameters usually means better knowledge and reasoning, but also more memory, slower loading and fewer tokens per second. On a CPU-only computer, speed is limited by memory bandwidth, so it drops roughly in proportion to model size. If the model does not fit in VRAM, part of it runs on the CPU and speed collapses. Quantization (4-bit here) is what makes 4B-parameter models fit in 3–4 GB. The right model is the **smallest one that is good enough for the task** on the target hardware, so measurements like these matter more than public leaderboards.\n",
        "\"\"\"\n",
        "display(Markdown(discussion))"
      ],
      "execution_count": 53,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "display_data",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": [
              "<IPython.core.display.Markdown object>"
            ],
            "text/markdown": "\nOn this machine (`Linux`, 14 GB RAM, GPU used):\n\n- **Speed:** `qwen3.5:4b` generates 49.36 tokens/s vs 48.65 for `gemma3:4b`. Response time: 25.77 s (`qwen3.5:4b`) vs 24.38 s (`gemma3:4b`), including a cold load of 11.91 s and 6.77 s.\n- **Memory:** `qwen3.5:4b` occupies 3.14 GB once loaded vs 2.88 GB for `gemma3:4b` (disk: 3.32 GB vs 3.35 GB). The loaded size is larger than the file because of the context (KV cache) and runtime buffers.\n- **Quality:** best automatic score for `qwen3.5:4b` (5.0/5 on the prime-number task of Exercise 7).\n\n**Trade-off.** More parameters usually means better knowledge and reasoning, but also more memory, slower loading and fewer tokens per second. On a CPU-only computer, speed is limited by memory bandwidth, so it drops roughly in proportion to model size. If the model does not fit in VRAM, part of it runs on the CPU and speed collapses. Quantization (4-bit here) is what makes 4B-parameter models fit in 3–4 GB. The right model is the **smallest one that is good enough for the task** on the target hardware, so measurements like these matter more than public leaderboards.\n"
          },
