      "source": [
        "## Part 18 — Questions for the Report\n",
        "Q13 and Q14 are generated from your measurements in Parts 6 and 16. The answers are also inserted into `report.pdf`."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "id": "c107",
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/",
          "height": 1000
        },
        "id": "c107",
        "outputId": "fc88bf35-c6bb-4f69-dc29-c354e3d1b23b"
      },
      "source": [
        "import psutil\n",
        "smallest = bench_df.set_index(\"Model\")[\"Loaded size (GB)\"].idxmin()\n",
        "ram_total = round(psutil.virtual_memory().total / 1e9)\n",
        "\n",
        "ANSWERS = {\n",
        "\"Q1. What is Local AI?\":\n",
        "\"Local AI means running AI models (here a large language model) directly on your own computer or server instead of calling a \"\n",
        "\"provider's cloud API. The model weights are downloaded once and inference is done on the local CPU/GPU, so prompts, documents \"\n",
        "\"and answers never leave the machine, and the model works offline.\",\n",
        "\n",
        "\"Q2. Difference between a Cloud LLM and a Local LLM?\":\n",
        "\"A cloud LLM (e.g. GPT, Claude, Gemini) runs in the provider's data centre: you send your data over the Internet to an API, pay \"\n",
        "\"per token, need a connection and an API key, but get very large, frontier-level models without needing hardware. A local LLM runs \"\n",
        "\"on your machine: data stays private, it works offline, there is no per-token cost and you control the exact model version, but \"\n",
        "\"you are limited to smaller models that fit your RAM/GPU, which are usually less capable and can be slower; you also manage \"\n",
        "\"installation and updates yourself.\",\n",
        "\n",
        "\"Q3. What role does Ollama play?\":\n",
        "\"Ollama is the local model runtime and server. It downloads and stores models from its library (ollama pull), manages them \"\n",
        "\"(list, show, rm), loads them into RAM/VRAM and unloads them after a timeout, runs inference with an optimised engine based on \"\n",
        "\"llama.cpp (CPU, NVIDIA/AMD GPU, Apple Metal), applies each model's chat template, and exposes everything through a CLI and a \"\n",
        "\"REST API on localhost:11434 (/api/generate, /api/chat, /api/embed, /api/tags, /api/ps...). Any program can therefore use a \"\n",
        "\"local model with simple HTTP requests, like a cloud API.\",\n",
        "\n",
        "\"Q4. What is model quantization?\":\n",
        "\"Quantization stores the model's weights with fewer bits per number, for example 4-bit integers (Q4_K_M) instead of 16-bit \"\n",
        "\"floats. A 4-billion-parameter model goes from about 8 GB in FP16 to about 2.5-3 GB in 4-bit. This reduces memory and disk usage \"\n",
        "\"and speeds up inference (less data to move from memory), with a small loss of accuracy. Most Ollama models are distributed in \"\n",
        "\"4-bit quantization (shown as 'quantization' in Part 5).\",\n",
        "\n",
        "\"Q5. Why does model size affect memory consumption?\":\n",
        "\"To generate each token the model must use all of its weights, so all of them must be loaded in RAM or VRAM. Memory is roughly \"\n",
        "\"number of parameters x bytes per parameter: 4B parameters x 0.5 byte (4-bit) is about 2-2.5 GB, 9B is about 5-6 GB, plus the \"\n",
        "\"KV cache that grows with the context length and some runtime buffers. A larger model therefore needs more memory, and if it \"\n",
        "\"does not fit in the GPU it partly runs on the CPU, which is much slower.\",\n",
        "\n",
        "\"Q6. Why can a smaller model sometimes be preferable?\":\n",
        "\"A smaller model loads faster, generates more tokens per second, uses less RAM and energy, and runs on modest hardware (laptops, \"\n",
        "\"CPU-only machines, edge devices). For simple or narrow tasks - classification, extraction, summarising a supplied document, \"\n",
        "\"RAG over local files, structured JSON output - a small model is often good enough. Lower latency gives a better user \"\n",
        "\"experience, and several small models or many users can be served on the same machine. The best model is the smallest one that \"\n",
        "\"meets the quality requirement.\",\n",
        "\n",
        "\"Q7. Advantages of local inference?\":\n",
        "\"Privacy and confidentiality (data never leaves the machine - important for medical, legal or company data and GDPR); offline \"\n",
        "\"operation; no per-token cost or subscription; no rate limits; predictable latency without network round-trips; full control \"\n",
        "\"over the model version (results do not change because a provider updated the model); possibility to customise or fine-tune \"\n",
        "\"models; independence from a provider.\",\n",
        "\n",
        "\"Q8. Disadvantages of local inference?\":\n",
        "\"Local models are smaller and usually less capable than frontier cloud models (reasoning, knowledge, long context); performance \"\n",
        "\"depends on your hardware and can be slow on CPU; large models require expensive GPUs and a lot of RAM; models take several GB \"\n",
        "\"of disk; you must install, update and secure the software yourself; energy consumption is on your side; scaling to many users \"\n",
        "\"is harder than with a managed cloud service.\",\n",
        "\n",
        "\"Q9. Explain the architecture of your Python application.\":\n",
        "\"The FSTT Local AI Assistant has four layers. (1) Front-end: index.html with JavaScript sends the question with fetch() as JSON \"\n",
        "\"to POST /ask and displays the answer, the sources, the response time and tokens/s. (2) Back-end: a Flask app (app.py) bound to \"\n",
        "\"127.0.0.1. For each question it (3) calls the retriever (rag.py, a TF-IDF keyword retriever over the documents/ folder) to get \"\n",
        "\"the 2 most relevant documents, then builds the messages: a system prompt that forces answers from the context, the last 6 \"\n",
        "\"exchanges of the conversation history, and the context plus the question. (4) It sends them with requests to Ollama's \"\n",
        "\"/api/chat on localhost:11434, optionally with a JSON schema in 'format' for structured answers. Ollama runs the local LLM and \"\n",
        "\"returns the answer with token counts and durations; Flask stores the plain question and answer in the history, measures the \"\n",
        "\"time and returns JSON to the browser. chatbot.py is the console version with memory and benchmark.py measures speed and memory.\",\n",
        "\n",
        "\"Q10. Explain the role of the REST API.\":\n",
        "\"The REST API is the interface between applications and the model. Ollama exposes HTTP endpoints on localhost:11434 that accept \"\n",
        "\"and return JSON: /api/generate (single prompt), /api/chat (conversation with roles), /api/embed (embeddings), /api/tags and \"\n",
        "\"/api/ps (models on disk and in memory), /api/pull. Because it is plain HTTP + JSON, any language or tool (Python, Java, \"\n",
        "\"JavaScript, curl, Flask, Open WebUI) can use the model without knowing how it runs; the application and the model are \"\n",
        "\"decoupled, and the same code could target a remote server by changing the URL. Our own Flask app also exposes a small REST API \"\n",
        "\"(/ask, /reset) to the browser.\",\n",
        "\n",
        "\"Q11. What is RAG?\":\n",
        "\"Retrieval-Augmented Generation: before generating an answer, the system retrieves relevant passages from an external knowledge \"\n",
        "\"base (here local text files) and inserts them into the prompt as context, instructing the model to answer from that context. \"\n",
        "\"Pipeline: documents -> retriever (keywords/TF-IDF or embeddings with a vector index) -> relevant context -> LLM -> answer. It \"\n",
        "\"lets a model answer with private or up-to-date information it was never trained on, reduces hallucinations, allows citing \"\n",
        "\"sources, and avoids retraining the model when documents change. In Part 13, the question about the 2018 World Cup retrieved no \"\n",
        "\"document, so the assistant answered 'Information not available.'\",\n",
        "\n",
        "\"Q12. Difference between an LLM and a multimodal LLM?\":\n",
        "\"A standard LLM only processes text: text tokens in, text tokens out. A multimodal LLM also accepts other inputs such as images \"\n",
        "\"(sometimes audio or video). It adds an encoder for each modality - for images a vision transformer - and a projection layer that \"\n",
        "\"maps the image features into the LLM's embedding space as 'visual tokens', so the language model can reason over the image and \"\n",
        "\"the text together. This enables image description, visual question answering, OCR and document understanding (Part 14 used \"\n",
        "f\"{MODEL2}, which can read images).\",\n",
        "\n",
        "\"Q13. Compare two local models experimentally.\":\n",
        "\"See the benchmark table (Part 16) and the Exercise 7 table. \" + discussion.replace(\"**\", \"\").replace(\"`\", \"\").strip(),\n",
        "\n",
        "\"Q14. Which model would you deploy on a computer with limited RAM? Explain.\":\n",
        "f\"{smallest}, because it had the smallest memory footprint in my measurements \"\n",
        "f\"({bench_df.set_index('Model').loc[smallest, 'Loaded size (GB)']:.2f} GB loaded) on a machine with {ram_total} GB of RAM. \"\n",
        "\"On a computer with limited RAM the model must fit in memory together with the operating system and other applications; \"\n",
        "\"otherwise the system swaps to disk and generation becomes extremely slow, or the model cannot load at all. I would choose a \"\n",
        "\"small (1-4B parameter) instruct model in 4-bit quantization, keep the context window moderate (the KV cache also uses memory), \"\n",
        "\"load only one model at a time and set a short keep_alive so the memory is released when idle. If the quality is not \"\n",
        "\"sufficient, RAG over local documents is a cheaper improvement than a bigger model; with very little RAM (below 8 GB) I would \"\n",
        "\"move down to qwen3.5:2b or gemma3:1b.\",\n",
        "\n",
        "\"Q15. What security risks exist when exposing a local LLM server to a network?\":\n",
        "\"Ollama has no authentication by default. If it is exposed (OLLAMA_HOST=0.0.0.0, port 11434 open on a LAN or the Internet), \"\n",
        "\"anyone who can reach the port can: use your compute and electricity for free (unauthorized use, denial of service by flooding \"\n",
        "\"long requests); list, pull, delete or overwrite models (/api/tags, /api/pull, /api/delete), including loading malicious or \"\n",
        "\"unwanted models; read data sent by other users if logs or shared applications leak it; and exploit possible vulnerabilities of \"\n",
        "\"the server - thousands of exposed Ollama instances have been found on the Internet. At the application level there is also \"\n",
        "\"prompt injection: a document in the RAG folder or a user message can contain instructions that make the model ignore its system \"\n",
        "\"prompt or reveal other documents, and the Flask app itself has no login or rate limit. Mitigations: keep Ollama and the app \"\n",
        "\"bound to 127.0.0.1 (as in our app.py); if remote access is needed, put a reverse proxy (Nginx/Caddy) in front with \"\n",
        "\"authentication and TLS (HTTPS), restrict access with a firewall or VPN, add rate limiting and request size limits, keep Ollama \"\n",
        "\"updated, only use trusted models and documents, and treat model output as untrusted (never execute it automatically).\",\n",
        "}\n",
